<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> My Evening Routine</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I'm Sleepy. It's Bedtime.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Express likes and dislikes. Interactive: Respond to simple instructions during activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can express preferences about bedtime (e.g., "I like bedtime stories."). Can express preferences about personal bedtime routines (e.g., "I like to brush my teeth before I go to bed."). Can follow and respond to instructions about bedtime (e.g., "Go to bed now." "Okay.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards of various evening routine actions (e.g., *brush teeth*, *wear pajamas*, *read story*, *go to bed*). The teacher asks, "What do you do before bed?" and encourages students to point to flashcards and say single words or short phrases like "Brush teeth!" or "Read book!" The teacher can sing a simple "It's Bedtime" song or chant that uses the target vocabulary and "I'm sleepy."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a picture of a child reading a *book* and says, "I like *bedtime stories*." Then, the teacher holds up a *toothbrush* and says, "I like to *brush* my *teeth*." The teacher models the pronunciation of /b/ in "*bed*" and /t/ in "*teeth*", emphasizing the sounds. The teacher also demonstrates blending for /w-ɒ-ʃ/ in "*wash*" and /t-iː-θ/ in "*teeth*" while performing the actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher uses visuals and gestures to check understanding. "Is this a *pillow*?" (Students: "Yes!"). "Do I *yawn* when I'm *sleepy*?" (Students: "Yes!"). "Do I like to *wash* my *face* in the morning or at *night*?" (Students point to "night" or say "Night!"). The teacher shows a picture of a *dark* room and asks, "Is the room *dark* or *light*?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated dialogue projected on the board or printed on large cards, depicting a child and a parent discussing evening routines. The dialogue includes phrases like "It's *bedtime*," "I am *sleepy*," "First, I *brush* my *teeth*," "Then, I *wear* my *pajamas*," "Can you *read* me a *bedtime story*, please?", and "Goodnight, *sleep* well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student understands and responds to simple questions about daily routines and expresses personal needs politely.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listen and Point:</b> The teacher reads the dialogue aloud. Students listen and point to the corresponding pictures (e.g., "Point to the child brushing *teeth*.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher makes simple statements based on the dialogue. Students say "True" or "False." (e.g., "The child *washes* her *face* *first*. True or False?" "The child is *tired*. True or False?").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Expressing likes and dislikes using "I like..." and "I don't like..." to describe preferences for evening routines. Using sequencing words (*First, then, next, last*) and present simple for daily routines. Incorporating vocabulary: *bed, pajamas, teeth, toothbrush, pillow, blanket, story, book, clock, night, water, face, brush, wash, wear, sleep, read, yawn, close, dream, sleepy, tired, clean, quiet, dark, comfortable*.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Frames:</b> The teacher displays sentence frames like "I like to ______." and "I don't like to ______." with pictures of various evening activities/items. Students practice saying sentences chorally, then individually, filling in the blanks orally (e.g., "I like to *read* a *book*.", "I don't like to go to *bed* early."). The teacher provides clear examples using target vocabulary such as *pajamas*, *story*, *brush teeth*, *wash face*.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled - Sequencing Routine Cards:</b> The teacher provides sets of picture cards showing evening routine steps (e.g., *wash face*, *brush teeth*, *wear pajamas*, *read book*, *go to bed*). In small groups, students arrange the cards in order and take turns describing the routine using sequencing words: "*First*, I *wash* my *face*. *Then*, I *brush* my *teeth*. *Next*, I *wear* my *pajamas*. *Last*, I go to *bed*." The teacher circulates and provides support and error correction, focusing on pronunciation of /b/ and /t/ sounds.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - "Do You Like...?" Survey:</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a simple list of bedtime activities/items (e.g., *bedtime stories*, *dark room*, *soft pillow*, *cold water*, *long dreams*). Students take turns asking their partner, "Do you like (activity/item)?" The partner responds, "Yes, I like (activity/item)." or "No, I don't like (activity/item)." The teacher encourages them to add "because I am *sleepy*" or "because it's *comfortable*."</li>
                </ol>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "My Evening Routine".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Role-play: "My Bedtime Story Request and Routine Sharing".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme:</b> Students work in pairs. One student plays the role of a child who is *sleepy* and getting ready for *bed*. The other student plays a parent or older sibling. The child must: 1) describe at least two steps of their evening routine using sequencing words (*First*, *then*). 2) express a preference using "I like..." or "I don't like...". 3) politely ask for a *bedtime story* using "Can you read me a *bedtime story*, please?". The parent/sibling must respond and say "Goodnight, *sleep* well!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Actions:</b> The teacher provides a simple prompt card with key phrases and visuals for support. The teacher observes and encourages natural interaction, focusing on fluency and communicative success over strict accuracy. Examples: "First, I *wear* my *pajamas*. *Then*, I *brush* my *teeth*. I like a *quiet* *story*. Can you *read* me a *bedtime story*, please?"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their short role-play (approx. 1-2 minutes per pair) for another pair or the class. The teacher uses a simple checklist to note student performance.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of /b/ (bed, blanket) and /t/ (teeth, tired).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses at least two sequencing words (*First, then, next, last*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Expresses preferences using "I like..." or "I don't like...".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Includes at least three target vocabulary words (e.g., *pajamas*, *brush teeth*, *read story*, *sleepy*).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates polite interaction (e.g., "Can you read me... please?").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I learned to say: _________." (e.g., "I like bedtime stories.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I like about my *bedtime* is: _________." (e.g., "my *comfortable blanket*.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 7-8 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me today when talking about my evening routine?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was difficult for me when talking about my evening routine? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Most students were able to use "I like..." and "First, then..." to describe routines. Some struggled with blending sounds in "wash" and "teeth" and consistent use of politeness markers. Next time, I will provide more explicit drilling for challenging pronunciations and incorporate quick role-play variations to reinforce politeness. The use of visuals and sentence frames was highly effective for this grade level. Some students needed more encouragement to produce longer sentences than just single words.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Great job speaking about your evening routines and what you like! Next lesson, we will use these same words and phrases to *write* about our own evening routines. We will make our own bedtime routine story!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 0;">Date: ___ Time: 3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>